@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U02:L01")
{
    @* First-party Grade7 proportional reasoning lesson, no source artwork. *@
    <section class="rich-lesson-section rich-lesson-section--g7-proportional-reasoning"
             data-g7-u02-l01 aria-label="Grade 7 original equivalent-ratio investigation">
      <h3>One of These Things Is Not Like the Others — Edulytiks</h3>
      <h4>1.1 Build a labeled double number line</h4>
      <p>Complete aligned top ticks 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 and
         bottom ticks 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. Invent a context
         with TWO different quantities, label both
         with correct units, and explain the shared
         multiplicative relationship and how 10
         on the top corresponds to 5 on the bottom.
         A teacher should evaluate your invented context.</p>
      <h4>1.2 Compare first-party mystery drink mixtures</h4>
      <p>Consider three hypothetical drink recipes:
         A: 1 cup water and 1.5 teaspoons of powder;
         B: 2 cups water and 0.5 teaspoon of powder;
         C: 1 cup water and 0.25 teaspoon of powder.
         Explain which recipe would have the strongest
         concentration under equal ingredients, and
         why B and C are equivalent at 0.25 teaspoon
         per cup despite differing total amounts.
         Construct a ratio table or a double number line
         for both recipes. Any real tasting,
         classroom supplies, food restrictions and
         discussion remain teacher-managed.</p>
      <p>Extension: A hypothetical salt-and-sugar mixture
         uses water/salt/sugar amounts 2 cups/4 tsp/0.25 cups;
         a second uses 1.5 cups/3 tsp/0.2 cups;
         a third uses 1 cup/2 tsp/0.125 cups.
         Compare BOTH salt and sugar concentration per
         cup water. The first and third match in both
         components; the middle differs in sugar ratio.</p>
      <h4>1.3 Draw and explain scaled crescent-like figures</h4>
      <p>Using the first-party crescent reference above,
         compare A with B and C. B grows by a factor
         1.5 on BOTH width and height; C grows by
         1.5 in width only. Explain why matching
         just one ratio does not establish
         a uniform scaled copy. Draw two figures,
         mark corresponding maximum widths and heights,
         then represent their measurements in a table
         or on a double number line. Have your teacher
         review the original drawings and argument.</p>
      <details><summary>Open mathematical checks after reasoning</summary>
        <p>For the line, 2 top units align with 1 bottom
           unit and 10 align with 5. For the recipes:
           A=1.5 tsp/cup, B=0.25, C=0.25;
           B and C are equivalent. In the salt/sugar
           extension, mixtures A and C have equal
           ratios for BOTH ingredients; B's sugar
           concentration differs. Uniform crescent
           scale A to B is 1.5 in both dimensions;
           A to C is NOT uniform.</p>
      </details>
      <h4>Cool-down — independent Edulytiks assessment</h4>
      <p>A color-mixing recipe uses 3 units of blue for
         every 2 units of yellow. Compare it with
         a second recipe using 9 blue and 6 yellow,
         and a third using 9 blue and 8 yellow.
         Decide which mixture has the same proportions.
         Draw a double number line showing
         the equivalent ratios and give a short
         written explanation of why the third
         recipe differs. Your teacher evaluates
         the representation and reasoning.</p>
      <p><strong>Assessment boundary:</strong> Exact ratio
         answers can be machine checked; original
         drawings, real mixtures, learner-invented
         line contexts and verbal justification
         require human review. No learner
         artefact or teacher score is saved by
         this view, and the publisher's gated
         Cool-down has not been reproduced.</p>
    </section>
}
@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U02:L02")
{
    <section class="rich-lesson-section rich-lesson-section--g7-proportion-tables"
             data-g7-u02-l02 aria-label="Grade 7 tables and proportional constants">
      <h3>Introducing Proportional Relationships with Tables</h3>
      <h4>2.1 Notice, question and test an inconsistent table</h4>
      <p>In a hypothetical towel shipment, a table records
         1 case → 12 rolls, 2 cases → 36 rolls,
         5 cases → 60 rolls, 10 cases → 120 rolls.
         Notice and discuss what might be inconsistent.
         Compute every rolls-per-case ratio, and
         identify the row that prevents a constant
         of proportionality. If each case contained
         12 rolls, 2 cases would require 24 rolls,
         not 36. Do not ignore the inconsistent entry.</p>
      <h4>2.2 Feeding a crowd — two proportional tables</h4>
      <p>A first-party recipe requires 2 cups of rice
         per 6 people. Complete a ratio table containing
         2 cups/6 people, 3 cups/9 people,
         10 cups/? people, and ? cups/45 people.
         Next a batch of 6 spring rolls serves 3 people.
         Extend its table for 30 and 40 rolls, and
         calculate how many rolls are needed for
         28 people. Explain the constant of
         proportionality for each chosen column order,
         and why reversing columns reciprocates it.</p>
      <h4>2.3 Bread dough — fractional constant</h4>
      <p>An original class example uses 8 tablespoons
         of honey for every 10 cups of flour.
         Complete the table for 20 tablespoons
         of honey, 13 tablespoons and 20 cups of
         flour. Write and interpret the constant
         5/4 cups of flour per tablespoon of honey,
         and the inverse 4/5 tablespoon of
         honey per cup of flour. Use diagrams,
         tables and a written explanation.</p>
      <h4>2.4 Quarters and dimes</h4>
      <p>Four quarters have the same value as ten dimes.
         Create the pairs (1,2.5), (4,10),
         (6,15) and (14,35).
         Explain the meaning of 2.5 dimes per quarter
         as a VALUE equivalent, rather than claiming
         a shop pays with half a coin. A teacher
         reviews your table and reasoning.</p>
      <h4>Additional practice and transfer</h4>
      <p>Draw a table for chocolate milk:
         2 cups milk for 3 tablespoons syrup.
         Determine syrup for 8 and 10 cups and
         state the constant with units.
         Separately compare 3 cups of red paint
         per 7 cups of white paint, and
         the ratio cards 4:7, 8:15, 16:28,
         2:3 and 20:35. Identify all ratios
         equivalent to 4:7 and justify the grouping.
         Apply previous scale-drawing area rules
         where appropriate, without replacing
         this lesson's proportionality work.</p>
      <details><summary>Check numerical answers after your work</summary>
        <p>Rice: 10 cups serve 30 people;
           45 people need 15 cups.
           Spring rolls: 30 rolls serve 15 people,
           40 rolls serve 20, and 28 people need
           56 rolls. Bread dough: 20 tbsp honey
           use 25 cups flour, 13 tbsp use 16.25 cups,
           and 20 cups flour need 16 tbsp honey.
           Quarters-to-dimes factor is 5/2.
           Chocolate syrup-per-milk factor is 3/2,
           red paint-per-white factor is 3/7.
           Matching ratio cards are A, C and E.</p>
      </details>
      <h4>Cool-down — independently authored Edulytiks task</h4>
      <p>One maker uses 6 meters of string to assemble
         4 identical decorations. Another makes
         10 decorations with the same design.
         Complete a table for 1, 4, 7 and
         10 decorations, and state both the
         meters-per-decoration constant and
         its inverse with suitable units.
         Explain why changing the first
         column from 4 to 10 is not the same
         as changing the fixed unit rate.</p>
      <p><strong>Assessment boundary:</strong> Numeric
         results are exact Practice; the source
         table anomaly, written interpretations,
         invented contexts and hand-drawn tables
         need teacher review. No third-party
         figures or gated original Cool-down
         have been reproduced.</p>
    </section>
}
@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U02:L03")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section rich-lesson-section--g7-conversion-constants"
           data-g7-u02-l03 aria-label="Grade 7 reciprocal unit rate tables">
    <h3>More about Constant of Proportionality — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>3.1 Equivalent measurements</h4>
    <p>Prepare pairs of equal measurements using mixed
       time, feet, inches, centimeters and meters:
       30 minutes = 1/2 hour, 12 inches = 1 foot,
       0.4 m = 40 cm, and 0.01 m = 1 cm.
       Make a matching-card collection and justify
       at least four unit conversions to a partner.</p>
    <h4>3.2 Centimeters and millimeters: two directions</h4>
    <p>Construct one table from centimeter values
       <strong>9, 12.5, 50 and 88.49</strong> to
       millimeters; then reverse for millimeter
       values <strong>70, 245, 4 and 699.1</strong>.
       Report both constants with their physical
       units: 10 mm/cm, and 1/10 cm/mm.
       Explain why the inverse constant is not 10,
       although dividing by 10 produces the
       correct numerical conversion.
       Extend the work to areas: 1 cm² = 100 mm²,
       not 10 mm². Draw a squared-grid
       explanation for teacher review.</p>
    <h4>3.3 A plane at constant speed</h4>
    <p>A first-party flight-table illustration follows
       the route Pittsburgh → Saint Louis →
       Albuquerque → Phoenix. An aircraft flies
       550 miles in 1 hour, then 1 hour and
       42 minutes on the next leg. At the same
       speed, calculate the second distance.
       If the following leg is 330 miles, determine
       its flying time in minutes. Explain how
       550 miles/hour and 55/6 miles/minute
       represent the same rate in different units,
       and what happens to the constant when
       the columns change from time→distance
       to distance→time. Do not reproduce
       a third-party map graphic.</p>
    <h4>Practice transfer and review</h4>
    <p>Complete a runner's table at 6 miles/hour;
       convert kilometers and meters in both
       directions; contrast 12 inches/foot with
       1/12 foot/inch; calculate an area scale
       when 25,000 square miles correspond
       to 10 square inches on a plan. Compare
       first-party nested ovals whose outer,
       inner and vertical measurements are
       L=(5,3,4), M=(10,4,8), N=(15,9,12).
       Which changes uniformly?</p>
    <details><summary>Numerical self-check</summary>
      <p>9 cm = 90 mm, 12.5 cm = 125 mm,
         50 cm = 500 mm, 88.49 cm = 884.9 mm.
         70 mm = 7 cm, 245 mm = 24.5 cm,
         4 mm = 0.4 cm, 699.1 mm = 69.91 cm.
         1 h 42 min = 1.7 h, giving 935 miles.
         At 550 mph, 330 miles take 36 min.
         A real/drawn area ratio of 2500 yields
         a linear scale of 1 drawing inch to
         50 real miles. Oval M is not uniformly
         scaled relative to L; N is.</p>
    </details>
    <h4>Cool-down — independent Edulytiks task</h4>
    <p>A bicycle travels 21 kilometers in 1.5 hours
       at a constant speed. Find the constant
       kilometers/hour and its reciprocal
       hours/kilometer. Use the same relationship
       to calculate the distance in 2.25 hours
       and time for 35 km. Represent the
       quantities in a table with correct units
       and justify the inverse relationship.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Original
       unit-bearing tables, physical measurement,
       drawing and flight-map interpretation need
       human review. Exact arithmetic alone
       cannot approve the complete activity
       or a gated publisher Cool-down.</p>
  </section>
}
@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U02:L04")
{
  @* First-party Grade 7 U02 L04 mathematical tasks. Teacher must review
     open-ended reasoning, diagram and equation-writing. No publisher artwork. *@
  <section class="rich-lesson-section rich-lesson-section--g7-proportional-equations"
           data-g7-u02-l04 aria-label="Grade 7 proportional equations">
    <h3>Proportional Tables and Equations — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>4.1 Mental calculation and division</h4>
    <p>Find 645 ÷ 100, 645 ÷ 50, and 48.6 ÷ 30 mentally.
       Explain how one division calculation helps with another.
       Choose a positive divisor x for 48.6 ÷ x and justify
       why your answer is efficient rather than arbitrary.</p>
    <h4>4.2 Write equations from recipe tables</h4>
    <p>A community kitchen uses 2 cups of dry rice to prepare
       6 servings. Complete a table for 1, 2, 3, 12 and
       43 cups, and for x cups. State the servings-per-cup
       constant and write a proportional equation connecting
       cups x to servings y. Use a second table in which
       6 spring rolls provide 3 servings: find values for
       1, 10, 16 and 25 rolls, then write an equation for
       n rolls. Explain why the two coefficients differ.</p>
    <h4>4.3 Rate equation for travel</h4>
    <p>A plane travels 915 miles in 1.5 hours at constant speed.
       Fill a table of distances at 1, 1.5, 2 and 2.5 hours,
       then derive d = kt where d is distance in miles
       and t is elapsed time in hours. Calculate distances
       for 3 and 3.5 hours. Label units and explain why
       this proportional relationship passes through the origin.</p>
    <h4>4.4 Bread ingredients and variable direction</h4>
    <p>For every 8 tablespoons of honey, a bakery uses
       10 cups of flour. Complete a table at 1, 8, 16
       and 20 tablespoons, and for a variable h.
       Derive f = kh, where flour cups f depend on
       honey tablespoons h. Calculate flour needed at
       h = 15 and h = 17. Write the inverse relationship
       when flour is the input. Explain why switching the
       variables changes the coefficient.</p>
    <h4>Independent first-party practice</h4>
    <p>A workshop processes 7 kilograms of modeling material every 2 hours at a
       fixed rate. Represent the total material mass y (kilograms) and
       hours x with an equation y = kx.
       Make a table for x = 0, 2, 4 and 6 and determine
       the mass processed in 9 hours. Compare with a
       wrong proposed rule y = 7x + 2 and explain
       why a nonzero intercept cannot represent
       the stated proportional relationship.</p>
    <details>
      <summary>Review the computations after completing the tasks</summary>
      <p>Division: 6.45; 12.9; 1.62.
         Kitchen rice y = 3x. Rolls y = x/2.
         Plane d = 610t; at 2, 2.5, 3, 3.5 h:
         1220, 1525, 1830, 2135 miles.
         Bakery f = (5/4)h; for 15 and 17 tbsp,
         f = 75/4 and 85/4 cups; inverse h = (4/5)f.
         Workshop y = (7/2)x, so 9 hours yields 31.5
         kilograms of material; this continuous quantity
         can have fractional values.</p>
    </details>
    <h4>Cool-down — independent Edulytiks assessment</h4>
    <p>A garden supplier fills 4 planters using 7.5 liters
       of potting mix. Assuming a constant amount per
       planter, write y = kx for liters y needed
       for x planters. Fill a table for 0, 2, 4, 6
       and 10 planters. State and explain the coefficient
       as a unit rate, then reverse the relationship
       to express planters as a function of liters.
       A teacher reviews the equations, table,
       explanation and units.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review required:</strong> Check equation
       direction, table completeness, conceptual reasoning
       and written justifications; auto-graded numeric
       Practice is only supporting evidence. This activity
       does not claim to reproduce the inaccessible
       publisher assessment or automatically grade student work.</p>
  </section>
}
@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U02:L05")
{
  @* First-party Grade7 reciprocal-equation lesson, human-reviewed constructions. *@
  <section class="rich-lesson-section rich-lesson-section--g7-reciprocal-relationships"
           data-g7-u02-l05 aria-label="Grade 7 two equations for a proportion">
    <h3>Two Equations for a Relationship — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>5.1 Growing mosaic patterns</h4>
    <p>Create a two-row tile mosaic. A block contains 3 yellow
       and 2 blue tiles in each row. Draw the first and third
       patterns when 1 and 3 blocks are connected, then
       describe the 10th pattern. Explain the difference
       between additive changes between patterns and
       multiplicative relationships within a fixed pattern.
       A teacher checks your drawings and color counts.</p>
    <h4>5.2 Meters and centimeters in both directions</h4>
    <p>Complete two conversion tables: meters to centimeters
       for 0.94, 1.67 and 57.24 meters, and centimeters
       to meters for 250, 78.2 and 123.9 centimeters.
       Write <strong>c = 100m</strong> and
       <strong>m = (1/100)c</strong>; explain
       the constant and its reciprocal. Extend to
       cubic units: why does 1 cubic meter contain
       1,000,000 cubic centimeters, not 100?</p>
    <h4>5.3 Filling a water cooler</h4>
    <p>A water cooler fills with 8 gallons in 5 minutes
       at a uniform flow rate. Let w be gallons and
       t be minutes. Write both w = (8/5)t and
       t = (5/8)w. Explain what 8/5 and 5/8 measure.
       The flow later changes to 1 gallon every 3 minutes:
       write both new equations and compare flow speed.
       Support the comparison with a table or double
       number line and units.</p>
    <h4>5.4 Feeding aquarium shrimp</h4>
    <p>One shrimp receives 1/5 gram three times a day.
       Complete food totals for 1, 7 and 30 days.
       Write a forward equation for total food f in grams
       and days d and its inverse. Consider 10 shrimp:
       find daily food mass, then determine how many
       days 300 grams will last at this constant rate.
       Label tables, equations and the meaning of each
       reciprocal coefficient.</p>
    <h4>Independent transfer: meters and kilometers</h4>
    <p>At a local community event, runners cover 3.5 km.
       Write equations converting kilometers k to meters m
       and meters to kilometers. A teacher should confirm
       the direction and unit labels before accepting work.</p>
    <details><summary>Numerical checks after independent work</summary>
      <p>Meter-to-centimeter results: 94, 167 and 5724 cm.
         Centimeter-to-meter results: 2.5, 0.782 and 1.239 m.
         Water: 1.6 gallons/min or 0.625 min/gallon;
         new flow 1/3 gallon/min is slower.
         Each shrimp eats 3/5 g daily, 21/5 g in 7 days,
         and 18 g in 30 days. Ten shrimp require 6 g/day,
         so 300 g lasts 50 days. 3.5 km is 3500 m.</p>
    </details>
    <h4>Cool-down — independent Edulytiks assessment</h4>
    <p>A science club uses 6 liters of dye for 15 identical
       fabric panels. Let L be liters and p be panels.
       Derive both L = kp and p = hL,
       interpret k and h with units, and calculate
       the amount for 22.5 panels as a continuous
       mathematical model. Discuss how an actual
       production order would handle whole panels.
       Provide a table and explanations for teacher review.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Inspect original
       diagrams, models, tables, explanations and appropriate
       units. Numeric checks do not certify the complete
       lesson or reproduce an inaccessible original Cool-down.</p>
  </section>
}
@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U02:L06")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section rich-lesson-section--g7-equation-problems"
           data-g7-u02-l06 aria-label="Grade 7 using proportional equations">
    <h3>Use Equations to Solve Problems — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>6.1 Reason about decimal quotients</h4>
    <p>Without first calculating, arrange 42.6 ÷ 0.07,
       42.6 ÷ 70, 42.6 ÷ 0.7 and 426 ÷ 70
       from least to greatest. Explain the powers of ten.
       Complete 42.6 ÷ 7 with the decimal point in
       the correct place, then use the relationship
       to check the four divisions.</p>
    <h4>6.2 Tickets, revenue and a realistic count</h4>
    <p>A first-party theater model expects $311,000 from
       5,000 equally priced tickets. Derive a price
       and the equation R = kt. Predict revenue at
       7,000, 10,000, 50,000 and 120,000 tickets,
       and for an arbitrary t. Solve for t when
       R = $404,300. Decide how many whole tickets
       must be sold to earn at least $5,000,000;
       distinguish the exact fractional mathematical
       answer from a realizable whole-ticket count.</p>
    <h4>6.3 Recycled cans and mass</h4>
    <p>Ten aluminum cans weigh 0.16 kg and are worth
       $0.14 in recyclable material. How many cans
       weigh 2.4 kg, and what are they worth?
       Use w for mass in kilograms, c for number
       of cans and r for recycling dollars. Write
       and explain c = 62.5w, r = 0.014c and
       r = 0.875w. Explain why the coefficients'
       units matter, and how each equation is derived.</p>
    <h4>Transfer practice</h4>
    <p>A car travels at 65 miles per hour: calculate
       its distance in 1.5 hours and the time for
       26 miles. Next, connect bottle count b
       to total water volume w when each bottle
       holds 17 fluid ounces. Compare forward
       and reverse equations and units.</p>
    <details><summary>Check computed quantities</summary>
      <p>42.6/70 ≈ 0.60857, 426/70 ≈ 6.0857,
         42.6/0.7 ≈ 60.857 and 42.6/0.07 ≈ 608.57.
         Ticket price: $62.20. 6,500 tickets yield
         $404,300; to earn at least $5,000,000,
         80,386 whole tickets are required (earning
         $5,000,009.20). Recycling: 150 cans weigh
         2.4 kg and earn $2.10.
         A car drives 97.5 miles in 1.5 hours,
         and 26 miles takes 0.4 hour.</p>
    </details>
    <h4>Cool-down — independent Edulytiks assessment</h4>
    <p>An art recycling club collects 120 glass containers,
       weighing 18 kg together. A recycling buyer offers
       $0.45 for every 3 kg at a fixed rate.
       Write mass as a function of the container count,
       and payout as a function of mass and container count.
       Calculate the payout for 36 kg and explain
       each coefficient with the correct units.
       The teacher reviews all model equations, reasoning
       and any assumptions about identical containers.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check
       modeled quantities, inverse operations,
       whole-item feasibility, explanations and
       independently created tables. Numeric
       answers are not a complete automatic grade
       and do not reproduce a gated assessment.</p>
  </section>
}
@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U02:L07")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section rich-lesson-section--g7-table-ratio-tests"
           data-g7-u02-l07 aria-label="Grade 7 comparing table proportionality">
    <h3>Compare Proportional and Nonproportional Tables — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>7.1 Invent contrasting fruit drinks</h4>
    <p>Mix 5 lemons, 2 cups water and 2 tablespoons honey as a
       baseline drink. Invent four new batches: larger and
       smaller batches with the same flavor, a stronger
       lemon taste and a weaker lemon taste. State quantities
       for each ingredient and explain which models preserve
       every ingredient ratio. Compare your proposals with
       another learner. A teacher reviews taste descriptions.</p>
    <h4>7.2 Entry fees are not a simple proportional rule</h4>
    <p>A public park charges $6 per vehicle plus $2 per
       passenger. Make a table for vehicles with 2, 4, 10
       and 50 passengers. Calculate each passenger's
       share if the price is divided equally. Write
       C = 6 + 2p for entrance cost C and passenger
       count p. Compare C/p across the rows and explain
       why this is not a proportional relationship,
       although a pay-per-person-only price could be.</p>
    <h4>7.3 Track runner pace tables</h4>
    <p>Two runners report elapsed minutes after 2, 4, 6
       and 8 laps. Runner A: 4, 9, 15 and 23 minutes.
       Runner B: 5, 10, 15 and 20 minutes. Compute minutes
       per lap in each row; identify whose observed
       times are consistent with a constant pace.
       Write an equation for the consistent table
       and explain what an unchanged observed ratio
       can and cannot establish about unobserved times.</p>
    <h4>Practice table comparisons</h4>
    <p>Compare two original smoothie price lists:
       Shop A offers 8 oz/$6, 12 oz/$9 and 16 oz/$12.
       Shop B offers 8 oz/$6, 12 oz/$8 and 16 oz/$10.
       Find price per ounce and decide whether
       each displayed table <em>could</em> reflect
       a proportional rule. Test a taxi with $1 for
       the first tenth mile and $0.10 for each
       additional tenth; find its cost at 0.9 mile
       and 3.1 miles. Explain the role of fixed fees.</p>
    <details><summary>Review numerical results after independent work</summary>
      <p>Park costs for 2, 4, 10, 50 people are
         $10, $14, $26, $106, respectively.
         Cost per person changes from $5 to
         $3.50 to $2.60 to $2.12, so not proportional.
         Runner B has a constant 2.5 min/lap;
         A's pace varies. Shop A's shown pairs
         all have $0.75/oz, so they are consistent
         but do not prove unseen prices follow.
         Shop B's ratios differ. Taxi fares at
         0.9 and 3.1 miles are $1.80 and $4.00.</p>
    </details>
    <h4>Cool-down — original Edulytiks table assessment</h4>
    <p>A delivery service charges $4 to dispatch a
       vehicle and $1.50 for each package. Construct
       a table for 0, 2, 4 and 8 packages and explain
       why the fee is not proportional to package count.
       Design a second fee rule that is proportional,
       write it as y = kx, and compare the two
       relationships by inspecting ratios and
       their values at zero packages.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check tables,
       original recipes, appropriate zero behavior,
       justifications and explanations of what
       finite observations can establish. Numeric
       classification is supporting Practice only.</p>
  </section>
}
@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U02:L08")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section rich-lesson-section--g7-compare-equations"
           data-g7-u02-l08 aria-label="Grade 7 comparing equations">
    <h3>Which Equations Model Proportions? — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>8.1 Notice geometric growth patterns</h4>
    <p>Construct rectangles with dimensions 1×2, 2×4,
       3×6 and 4×8 grid units. Record each rectangle's
       length, perimeter and area. Compare how
       perimeter and area change with the scale.
       Discuss why simply noticing a pattern
       does not prove one proportional equation
       for all the quantities.</p>
    <h4>8.2 More conversions: proportional or affine?</h4>
    <p>Use F=(9/5)C+32 to complete a Celsius-to-Fahrenheit
       table for C=0,4,20,175. Complete another table
       converting 1, 3.5, 8 and 10 inches to centimeters
       using c=2.54n. Explain which relationship is
       proportional and why starting at zero matters.
       Include correct temperature and length units.</p>
    <h4>8.3 Compare cube edges, area and volume</h4>
    <p>Build or draw cubes with sides of 3, 5, 9.5 and 2
       units. Make three tables: total edge length E,
       surface area A and volume V. Derive
       <strong>E=12s</strong>, <strong>A=6s²</strong> and
       <strong>V=s³</strong>. State which output is
       proportional to side length and which are not.
       Compare with a rectangular prism whose width
       is 10, height is 5 and varying length is L:
       why is its volume V=50L proportional to L?</p>
    <h4>8.4 Test equations with tables</h4>
    <p>For each input x=2,3,4,5, evaluate six
       first-party candidates:
       y=x+4, y=x/4, y=4x, y=4^x, y=4/x
       and y=x^4. Record y/x for each nonzero x.
       Identify exactly which two equations have
       a constant ratio, and explain algebraically
       why the others cannot be expressed as y=kx.</p>
    <h4>Practice and evidence boundaries</h4>
    <p>Classify these relationships: rope remaining
       L=120-x, final price including 8% sales tax
       t=1.08p, sharing m marbles between four people
       x=m/4, and square-base prism V=12s².
       Build a table for y=(5/2)x and compare it
       with y=3.2x+5. A teacher checks equations,
       complete tables and explanations, not just
       single numeric responses.</p>
    <details><summary>Independent computation check</summary>
      <p>At x=2, y=x/4 gives 0.5 and y=4x gives 8;
         these are the only two proportional rules
         among the six candidates. A cube's edge
         total is 12s while its surface area 6s²
         and volume s³ are not proportional to s.
         At s=3, E=36, A=54, V=27.
         F(0)=32 shows the offset, while c=2.54n
         passes through zero. Tax and four-way
         marble splitting are proportional; a
         leftover-rope length and square-base prism
         volume are not.</p>
    </details>
    <h4>Cool-down — independent Edulytiks assessment</h4>
    <p>A shipping box has a fixed 4 cm by 6 cm base,
       and adjustable height h in centimeters.
       Determine whether its volume is proportional
       to h and write the equation. Compare this
       with a cube of side h and explain why its
       volume cannot be written using a fixed
       k times h for all positive h.
       Include two numerical table rows and
       a labeled geometric drawing for teacher review.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check accurate
       physical construction, units, labeled tables,
       contextual interpretations and general
       justification. Number checks alone do not
       grade complete source activities.</p>
  </section>
}
@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U02:L09")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section rich-lesson-section--g7-info-gap"
           data-g7-u02-l09 aria-label="Grade 7 proportional information gap">
    <h3>Solving Problems with Missing Information — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>9.1 Ask the right question first</h4>
    <p>A runner moves at a constant but initially unknown speed.
       What information do you need to estimate a finish time?
       Only after proposing a question, reveal this original
       data: the race is 10,000 meters, starts at 9:15 a.m.
       and the runner covers 156.25 meters each minute.
       Determine the elapsed duration and finish clock time.
       Explain which data was essential and which was not.</p>
    <h4>9.2 Two-card cycling information gap</h4>
    <p>Work in pairs without revealing the data prematurely.
       Card A asks: How long does a cyclist need to cover
       90 km at a constant pace? Card B contains additional
       information in the collapsed panel below.
       The first learner must ask specific questions
       and justify why each datum is needed before
       the other learner reveals it. Exchange roles
       using a second, rain-related question with
       an agreed constant-pace assumption.</p>
    <details><summary>Data card — reveal after the learner asks</summary>
       <p>Card A's cyclist travels at 15 km/hour.
          For a second example, a cyclist rides
          12 km/hour and the storm begins after
          1 hour 45 minutes. Find how far the cyclist
          travels before the storm, with units.
          Discuss what would change if speed varied.</p>
    </details>
    <h4>9.3 Compare worker totals and rates</h4>
    <p>Four volunteers review comments at constant rates.
       Person 1 processes 50,000 in 210 minutes;
       Person 2 processes 1,325 in each 5-minute period
       for 200 minutes; Person 3 follows c=331t
       for 120 minutes; Person 4 follows t=(3/800)c
       for 150 minutes. Compute each person's
       total and exact comments-per-minute rate.
       Rank them twice: by total completed and
       by speed. Explain why the rankings differ
       and support each with a table and equations.</p>
    <h4>Practice: is the model justified?</h4>
    <p>Compare the weight of identical paper sheets
       with the weight of randomly sized books.
       Explain which relation could have one exact
       weight-per-item coefficient. Model the number
       of batteries in packages containing exactly
       two batteries per toy. Contrast with sibling
       ages three years apart, where adding a
       constant difference is not proportional.</p>
    <details><summary>Numerical self-check</summary>
       <p>The runner takes 64 minutes and finishes at
          10:19 a.m. The 90-km cyclist at 15 km/h
          takes 6 hours; at 12 km/h a rider travels
          21 km in 1 hour 45 minutes.
          Volunteer totals: 50,000; 53,000;
          39,720; 40,000. Totals rank 2,1,4,3;
          exact rates: 5000/21, 265, 331,
          800/3 comments/minute; speed rank
          3,4,2,1.</p>
    </details>
    <h4>Cool-down — independently authored Edulytiks task</h4>
    <p>An emergency supply van drives at a constant rate.
       Your partner's data card shows an 84-km route
       but hides the speed. Write the question
       needed to determine travel time. After
       learning the rate is 28 km/h, calculate
       the time and write d=kt. Describe how a
       delivery fee or a change in speed would
       affect model suitability. The teacher
       reviews the questions and mathematical
       explanations, not just the numeric result.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check information requests,
       why a constant rate is appropriate, math tables,
       two distinct rankings and oral explanations.
       This is independent first-party task authoring,
       not a reproduction of a gated assessment.</p>
  </section>
}
@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U02:L10")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section rich-lesson-section--g7-graph-slope"
           data-g7-u02-l10 aria-label="Grade 7 proportional graph investigations">
    <h3>Graph Tables and Proportional Relationships — Edulytiks</h3>
    <figure>
      <img src="/curriculum/us-ccss-g7/geometry/u02-l10-proportional-vs-offset.svg"
           alt="Two original graphs: A shows shirt price rising by eight dollars per shirt through zero; B shows y equals ten minus two x, a straight line with an offset."
           loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Compare a line through the origin with one that does not.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <h4>10.1 Plot coordinate pairs</h4>
    <p>Use grid paper to plot (0,10), (1,8), (2,6),
       (3,4), (4,2). Do the points lie on a straight
       line? Does that mean the relationship is
       proportional? Check the origin before deciding.</p>
    <h4>10.2 T-shirt table and graph</h4>
    <p>Shirts cost $8 each with no fixed fee.
       Make a two-column table of quantity x and
       dollar cost y for x = 0,1,2,3,4,5,6.
       Draw accurately scaled axes and graph the
       coordinate pairs. Explain why each plotted
       point satisfies y=8x, and why a line
       through (0,0) describes the relationship.</p>
    <h4>10.3 Match tables and graphs</h4>
    <p>Prepare original sets of graph/table cards:
       y=3x, y=10-2x, y=x² and y=(1/4)x.
       Plot at least four points for each model,
       classify the graphs and match their tables.
       Compare a line through the origin, a
       straight offset line, and a curved graph.
       With a partner, explain why merely passing
       through the origin is not sufficient if
       the graph is curved.</p>
    <p>For a produce-by-weight graph, blueberries
       cost $6 per pound. Explain why a fractional
       x=1.25 pounds is meaningful and its graph
       can be drawn as a continuous line. Compare
       whole-shirt quantities, which may naturally
       be represented by discrete points only.</p>
    <details><summary>Numerical self-check</summary>
      <p>The warm-up is y=10-2x, which is linear
         but not proportional. Shirts follow y=8x.
         A proportional graph is a line through
         the origin with the same output/input
         ratio at every nonzero input.
         A curved y=x² graph passes through the
         origin but is not proportional.
         At 1.25 pounds, blueberries cost $7.50.</p>
    </details>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4>
    <p>A bicycle parts shop sells $9 reflectors with
       no base fee. Draw at least four points
       that represent the number of reflectors
       and their total price, label both axes,
       then write y=9x. Construct a second
       comparison graph for a shop charging a
       $4 handling fee plus $9 per reflector.
       Explain which plotted relation is
       proportional, including the origin test.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check axis scales,
       labeled coordinate points, correct graphs,
       discrete vs continuous domain assumptions,
       card sorting, and oral explanations.
       Machine-scored Practice only checks
       numeric or symbolic subresults.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U02:L11")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section rich-lesson-section--g7-graph-interpretation"
           data-g7-u02-l11 aria-label="Grade 7 interpreting proportional graphs">
    <h3>Read a Proportional Graph — Edulytiks</h3>
    <figure>
      <img src="/curriculum/us-ccss-g7/geometry/u02-l11-point-k-context.svg"
           alt="A distance-time graph through the origin with labeled points at 1 second and 2.5 meters, 4 seconds and 10 meters, and 8 seconds and 20 meters."
           loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Every labeled point describes the same constant rate; the point with x = 1 exposes the unit rate directly.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <h4>11.1 Interpret a point before calculating</h4>
    <p>A delivery cart moves at a constant pace. Explain in words what the point
       (8,20) means when x is elapsed seconds and y is meters traveled.
       Then explain why the origin belongs to this graph.</p>
    <h4>11.2 Locate the constant of proportionality</h4>
    <p>Use (8,20) to calculate k = y/x. Locate (1,k), write d = kt,
       and use the equation to predict the distance after 6 seconds.
       State the units of k.</p>
    <h4>11.3 Connect a changed rate to the graph</h4>
    <p>The cart must cover the same 20 meters in 4 seconds instead of 8.
       Determine the new constant of proportionality and describe how the
       new line would compare with the original line on the same axes.</p>
    <details><summary>Numerical self-check</summary>
      <p>The original rate is 20/8 = 2.5 meters per second, so (1,2.5)
         lies on d=2.5t and d(6)=15. Covering 20 meters in 4 seconds
         gives 5 meters per second and a steeper line through the origin.</p>
    </details>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4>
    <p>A tank fills at a constant rate and contains 30 liters after
       4 minutes. Find k, identify the point (1,k), write V=kt,
       and determine the volume after 6 minutes. Explain what the
       coordinates of your 6-minute point mean in context and sketch
       the proportional line with labeled axes.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check the student's invented or drawn
       graph, axis labels, units and contextual explanations. Machine-scored
       Practice checks only exact numerical or symbolic subresults. This
       Cool-down is independently authored and is not a reproduction of
       the gated original assessment.</p>
  </section>
}
